WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 146: Rutherford B. Hayes and Lemonade Lucy: Reclaiming Executive Integrity (1877–1881)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.280
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our portrait of Rutherford B.

4
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.720
[Rebecca] Hayes by exploring his quiet, courageous crusade to clean up American politics.

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:26.320
[Rebecca] Standing firm against the powerful party bosses who had helped put him in office, Hayes fought for civil service reform, and with First Lady Lucy Hayes, he transformed the moral tone of official Washington.

6
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.120
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.360
[George] Rutherford Burchard Hayes was never a flamboyant politician.

8
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:48.560
[George] Born in Delaware, Ohio in 1822, just months after his father's death, Hayes was raised by his devoted mother and a prosperous uncle who funded his education at Kenyan College and Harvard Law School.

9
00:00:48.560 --> 00:01:00.800
[George] In Cincinnati, Hayes established himself as a skilled criminal defense lawyer who quietly volunteered his services to defend runaway slaves escaping across the Ohio River under the Fugitive Slave Act.

10
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.120
[George] When the Civil War erupted, Hayes enlisted as a major in the 23rd Ohio Volunteer Infantry.

11
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:18.480
[George] He was wounded five times in combat, had four horses shot out from under him, and rose to the rank of brevet major general through raw physical bravery at South Mountain and Cedar Creek.

12
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:29.360
[George] Elected to Congress in 1864 while still serving on the front lines, Hayes famously wrote, Any man who would leave the Army to electioneer for Congress ought to be scalped.

13
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:41.360
[George] Serving three terms as governor of Ohio, he earned a national reputation as an honest fiscal conservative who championed prison reform, public universities, and civil service exams.

14
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:49.680
[George] When he took the presidential oath in March 1877, he announced in his inaugural address a principle that shocked party chieftains.

15
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.120
[George] He serves his party best who serves his country best.

16
00:01:53.120 --> 00:02:05.400
[George] Hayes had voluntarily pledged to serve only a single four-year term, freeing himself from the corrupting pressures of re-election and giving him the moral freedom to challenge the political bosses who ruled Washington.

17
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:13.560
[George] In the late 19th century, American federal government employment operated on the unwritten law of the spoils system.

18
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:26.040
[George] Government jobs, from postmasters and lighthouse keepers to tax collectors, were treated as political booty distributed to party loyalists who kicked back a percentage of their salaries into party campaign war chests.

19
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:33.880
[George] The crown jewel and nerve center of this corrupt patronage apparatus was the New York Custom House, located on Wall Street.

20
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:46.840
[George] Collecting over 70% of all federal customs revenue for the entire nation, the Custom House employed more than 1,000 clerks and collectors, generating immense wealth and staggering political leverage.

21
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:55.160
[George] The undisputed emperor of this domain was Senator Roscoe Conkling of New York, leader of the stalwart faction of the Republican Party.

22
00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:07.160
[George] Conkling was a striking, arrogant peacock of a man, tall, muscular, with a prominent yellow quiff of hair, who dressed in velvet waistcoats, lilac trousers and white gaiters.

23
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:16.760
[George] Conkling treated politics as a game of ruthless power and patronage, sneering at civil service reformers as snivel service hypocrites and man-milliners.

24
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:22.680
[George] At the head of the Custom House, Conkling had installed his loyal lieutenant, Chester Alan Arthur.

25
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:37.400
[George] Arthur was an affable, impeccably dressed gentleman who ran the office not for public efficiency, but as a political clubhouse that extracted mandatory campaign assessments from federal clerks and rewarded party hacks with lucrative no-show jobs.

26
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.960
[George] In June 1877, Hayes launched his opening salvo.

27
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:51.360
[George] Following a damning investigation by the Jay Commission that uncovered systemic bribery, overstaffing, and political extortion inside the New York Custom House.

28
00:03:51.600 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] Hayes issued an executive order prohibiting federal employees from taking active roles in political party management, running campaign committees, or paying political assessments.

29
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.560
[George] Conkling was incensed, he considered the order a direct insult to his authority and commanded Chester A.

30
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.720
[George] Arthur and Naval Officer Alonso B.

31
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.600
[George] Cornell to defy the president's instructions.

32
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:23.920
[George] When Hayes politely requested their resignations, Arthur and Cornell flatly refused, boasting that the United States Senate would protect them under the Tenure of Office Act.

33
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.000
[George] Hayes accepted the challenge.

34
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:36.480
[George] In October 1877, he nominated two independent reformers, Theodore Roosevelt S.R., the philanthropic father of the future president, and L.

35
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.840
[George] Bradford Prince, to replace Arthur and Cornell.

36
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:50.240
[George] Roscoe Conkling mobilized the Senate to invoke senatorial courtesy, an unwritten rule that senators had the veto power over federal appointments in their home states.

37
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.240
[George] Conkling rallied his colleagues to reject Roosevelt's nomination.

38
00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.840
[George] Far from backing down, Hayes dug in his heels.

39
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.720
[George] I am right, Hayes wrote in his diary.

40
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.440
[George] The New York Custom House is a disgrace.

41
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.520
[George] I am determined to break up the senatorial oligarchy that usurps the appointing power of the executive.

42
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:17.920
[George] Hayes waited until Congress adjourned in the summer of 1878, suspended Arthur and Cornell from office, and appointed General Edwin A.

43
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.440
[George] Merritt as collector.

44
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:26.400
[George] When the Senate reconvened in December 1878, Conkling launched a desperate fight to reinstate Arthur.

45
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:37.320
[George] In a marathon seven-hour speech delivered on the Senate floor, Conkling raged against Hayes, pacing the aisles, waving documents and mocking the president with savage sarcasm.

46
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.640
[George] But Hayes had outmaneuvered him.

47
00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:48.680
[George] The president had cultivated moderate Republicans and formed a quiet alliance with reform-minded Southern Democrats who resented Conkling's Northern machine.

48
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:58.280
[George] On February 3, 1879, after weeks of intense debate, the Senate voted 33 to 24 to confirm Hayes's nominees.

49
00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:06.760
[George] It was a shattering personal and political defeat for Roscoe Conkling, and the first major victory for the modern civil service reform movement.

50
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:18.600
[George] For the first time since the Civil War, an American president had confronted the Senate party bosses on their own turf and successfully defended the constitutional appointing power of the executive branch.

51
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:29.240
[George] Collector Merritt immediately instituted open, competitive civil service examinations at the New York Custom House, doubling efficiency and drastically cutting expenses.

52
00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:44.040
[George] A young Theodore Roosevelt J.R., who had been heartbroken when his father was defeated by Conkling's machinations, watched the episode closely, absorbing a lifelong passion for civil service reform that would later define his own presidency.

53
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:53.320
[George] While Rutherford fought the spoilsman on Capitol Hill, his wife Lucy Webb Hayes transformed the social and cultural life of the executive mansion.

54
00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.120
[George] Lucy was extraordinary in her own right.

55
00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:05.160
[George] She was the first First Lady of the United States to hold a college degree, graduating from Wesleyan Female College in Cincinnati at age 18.

56
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:13.800
[George] Brilliant, warm, and deeply empathetic, Lucy was a staunch abolitionist and an active supporter of women's education and orphanages.

57
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:23.280
[George] During the Civil War, she had spent months in the camps of the 23rd Ohio tending to wounded soldiers, who affectionately nicknamed her Mother Lucy.

58
00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:32.320
[George] Upon entering the White House in 1877, Lucy and Rutherford made a controversial decision that sent shockwaves through Washington High Society.

59
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.960
[George] They banned all wine, liquor, and alcohol from White House functions.

60
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:49.520
[George] Hayes himself was not a total abstainer, but both he and Lucy were passionate supporters of the temperance movement, believing that public drunkenness was tearing families apart and corrupting government officials.

61
00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:58.240
[George] At state dinners, foreign diplomats and cabinet secretaries were offered lemonade, fruit punch, and mineral water instead of champagne.

62
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:07.360
[George] Washington Cynics scoffed, and the press quickly dubbed her Lemonade Lucy, a moniker she never used herself, but bore with graceful good humor.

63
00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:14.640
[George] When visiting diplomats complained that water flowed like champagne at the White House, Hayes defended his wife proudly.

64
00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.080
[George] Lucy was right.

65
00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:22.720
[George] In an age of excess, the president's house should set an example of sobriety, simplicity and dignity.

66
00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:33.520
[George] Despite the ban on alcohol, the Hayes White House was widely regarded as one of the warmest, most welcoming, and culturally vibrant in 19th century American history.

67
00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:46.640
[George] Lucy Webb Hayes modernized the interior of the mansion, bringing in the first telephone personally demonstrated by Alexander Graham Bell, and the first typewriter, and outfitting a dedicated White House Library.

68
00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:57.040
[George] She was the first First Lady to invite African American artists to perform in the East Room, hosting the renowned soprano Marie Selica Williams for a celebrated concert.

69
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:05.240
[George] Lucy also inaugurated what would become one of the most enduring traditions in American civic life, the White House Easter egg roll.

70
00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:19.640
[George] In 1878, after Congress passed a law banning local children from rolling Easter eggs on the landscaped terraces of Capitol Hill to protect the grass, Lucy invited Washington children to come to the White House South Lawn instead.

71
00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:31.000
[George] Hundreds of laughing children arrived on Easter Monday, rolling brightly colored hard-boiled eggs down the gentle grassy slopes while the president and First Lady mingled warmly among them.

72
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:42.760
[George] On Sunday evenings, the Hayes family gathered cabinet secretaries, congressmen, and visiting citizens in the blue room for lively gospel hymn sings, accompanied by the parlor piano.

73
00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:43.960
[George] When Rutherford B.

74
00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:53.400
[George] Hayes walked out of the White House in March 1881, he left the American presidency far stronger, cleaner, and more respected than he had found it.

75
00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:59.480
[George] He had kept his promise to serve only one term, refusing repeated calls to run for re-election.

76
00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:13.960
[George] He had broken the stranglehold of the senatorial spoils machine, revived the financial standing of the country by successfully resuming specie payments in gold, and demonstrated that the White House could govern with unassailable personal probity.

77
00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:29.160
[George] Hayes retired with Lucy to their beloved estate, Spiegel Grove, in Fremont, Ohio, where he devoted his remaining years to prison reform, veterans' welfare, and educational opportunities for black children in the South through the Slater Fund.

78
00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:38.520
[George] When Lucy died suddenly of a stroke in 1889, Hayes was inconsolable, writing in his diary, The light of my life has gone out.

79
00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] He passed away four years later, his last whispered words being, I know that I am going where Lucy is.

80
00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:00.800
[Rebecca] Rutherford B.

81
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:07.520
[Rebecca] Hayes proved that executive integrity and moral courage could withstand the fiercest political machines of the Gilded Age.

82
00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:14.400
[Rebecca] By breaking the spoil system at the New York Custom House, he laid the essential groundwork for the modern American civil service.

83
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:19.760
[Rebecca] Next week, we turned to the brilliant, tragic successor Hayes helped elect, James A.

84
00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:29.120
[Rebecca] Garfield, whose dazzling rise from a log cabin and canal boat was cut short by an assassin's bullet and an agonizing 79 day battle for life.

85
00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:33.200
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

